var e=1,t=`alts-re-01`,n=`alternatives`,r=`Real Estate`,i=`Buildings valued off the rent they produce, where the yield you divide by matters more than the rent itself.`,a=`core`,o=[`quant-tvm-02`],s=10,c=[`CAIA-L1`,`CAIA-L2`,`practical`],l=[{type:`concept`,id:`what-it-is`,body:`Commercial real estate produces rent, so it is valued much like any other income stream: take the income and divide by a required yield.

Two terms carry almost all the weight.

**[[noi|Net operating income]]** is the rent collected less the costs of operating the building — management, maintenance, insurance, property taxes. It excludes financing costs and excludes capital expenditure, which is what makes it comparable across buildings with different owners and different debt.

**[[cap-rate|Capitalisation rate]]** is NOI divided by value. It is the yield the market requires on that income. Rearranged, **value = NOI / cap rate**, which you will recognise as the [[perpetuity]] formula: an income stream divided by a rate.

That identity is the whole valuation framework. A building generating $6m of NOI at a 5.5% cap rate is worth about $109m. Everything else in real estate analysis is a refinement of those two numbers.`},{type:`intuition`,id:`cap-rate-is-the-risk`,body:`Because value is NOI divided by a small number, the denominator does the damage.

Take that $6m of NOI. At a 5.5% cap rate the building is worth $109.1m. Let the cap rate move to 6.5% — one percentage point, with the rent completely unchanged — and the value becomes $92.3m. **A 15.4% fall in value with no change whatsoever in the building or its tenants.**

This is the [[perpetuity]] sensitivity from the annuities topic, appearing in a real asset class. And it explains something that otherwise looks strange: real estate returns can be sharply negative in periods when rents were rising, because cap rates expanded faster than income grew.

What moves cap rates? Broadly the same things that move any [[discount-rate]] — the [[risk-free-rate|risk-free rate]], credit availability, and the risk premium investors require for the asset type. So **real estate is a rate-sensitive asset dressed as a physical one.** The building is tangible; the valuation is a discounted income stream, and it behaves accordingly.`},{type:`concept`,id:`the-styles`,body:`Real estate funds describe themselves along a risk spectrum, and the labels are reasonably standardised.

**Core.** Stabilised, well-let buildings in established locations with long leases to strong tenants. Low leverage. Most of the return is income. Closest to a bond in behaviour.

**Core-plus.** Core assets with a modest improvement angle — some leases to renew, light refurbishment.

**Value-add.** Buildings with a specific problem to solve: vacancy to fill, leases to re-gear, a refurbishment to complete. Moderate leverage. Return is split between income and capital appreciation from fixing the problem.

**Opportunistic.** Development, major repositioning, distressed purchases, or whole-portfolio situations. High leverage, little or no income during the work, and the return depends almost entirely on executing the plan and exiting well.

The useful thing about the spectrum is that it tells you **where the return is meant to come from**. A core fund earning most of its return from capital appreciation has been carried by cap rate compression rather than by doing what it said it would.`},{type:`table`,id:`environments`,caption:`What helps and what hurts`,headers:[`Environment`,`Effect`,`Why`],rows:[[`Falling rates and compressing cap rates`,`Helps most`,`Values rise on unchanged rent. Flatters the whole asset class regardless of asset management skill.`],[`Rental growth above inflation`,`Helps`,`Grows NOI, which is the numerator and the part a manager can actually influence.`],[`Available mortgage financing`,`Helps`,`Leverage magnifies equity returns and supports transaction volumes and therefore values.`],[`Rising rates and expanding cap rates`,`Hurts badly`,`Values fall on unchanged rent, and refinancing becomes more expensive at the same time.`],[`Structural demand shifts`,`Hurts`,`A sector can lose tenants permanently. Retail and offices have both experienced this, and no cap rate makes an empty building valuable.`],[`Credit withdrawal from property lending`,`Hurts badly`,`Refinancing risk on maturing mortgages, forced sales, and a bid side that disappears.`]]},{type:`concept`,id:`how-it-blows-up`,body:`**Cap rate expansion with leverage.** The classic failure. A property bought at 60% [[loan-to-value|loan-to-value]] that loses 25% of its value has lost 62% of its equity, with the building fully let and performing. Leverage plus a denominator move is the mechanism.

**Refinancing at maturity.** Property debt is typically shorter than the holding period, so it must be refinanced. A loan maturing into higher rates and lower valuations may not be refinanceable at all — requiring fresh equity or a sale at the worst moment. This is a timing risk that has nothing to do with asset quality.

**Structural obsolescence.** Some demand shifts do not revert. A building whose tenant base has permanently moved elsewhere, or which cannot meet new energy standards without major capital expenditure, has a valuation problem no market recovery repairs.

**Development risk in opportunistic strategies.** Cost overruns, delays and a market that has moved by the time the building is finished. No income during construction means no cushion.

**Valuation lag.** Appraisal-based valuations move slowly and are anchored to recent comparable transactions. When transaction volumes collapse, there are no comparables — so marks can stay stale for several quarters precisely when the market is repricing fastest.`},{type:`pitfall`,id:`noi-quality`,body:`**Not all NOI is equal, and the cap rate applied to it is supposed to reflect that.** A building let for fifteen years to a government tenant and one let for two years to a start-up can produce identical NOI today and are not remotely the same asset.

So look behind the number: **weighted average lease length**, tenant credit quality, how much of the income expires soon, and whether current rents are above or below market. A building let above market rent has NOI that will fall at renewal, and applying a market cap rate to inflated income overstates value twice over.

This is where the phrase "the cap rate looks attractive" most often misleads. A high cap rate can mean a cheap asset or an income stream the market does not believe will persist.`},{type:`onTheJob`,id:`on-the-job`,body:`**Decompose the return into income and capital.** For a core fund especially. If most of the return came from capital appreciation, it came from cap rate compression, which is a market outcome rather than a manager achievement.

**Ask about the debt maturity profile against the hold period.** Refinancing risk is a scheduling problem you can see in advance, and it is where otherwise sound portfolios get into trouble.

**Ask for weighted average lease length and tenant concentration.** Two numbers that tell you how durable the NOI is.

**Ask whether in-place rents are above or below market.** Below-market rents are embedded upside; above-market rents are a cliff at renewal that current NOI conceals.

**Treat appraisal-based marks with appropriate scepticism in a falling market.** When transactions stop, appraisals lose their anchor. A portfolio marked flat through a quarter in which nothing traded has not been validated by anything.

**Check the capital expenditure requirement.** NOI excludes capex, so a building needing substantial investment to remain lettable has an NOI figure that overstates what the owner actually receives.`},{type:`keyTakeaways`,id:`takeaways`,items:[`Value = NOI / cap rate. It is the perpetuity formula, and the small denominator is where the risk lives.`,`A one-point cap rate move on unchanged rent shifts value by roughly 15%. Real estate is a rate-sensitive asset dressed as a physical one.`,`Core, value-add and opportunistic tell you where the return is supposed to come from. Check whether it actually did.`,`Look behind NOI at lease length, tenant quality and whether rents are above or below market. A high cap rate can mean cheap, or disbelieved.`]}],u=[{id:`alts-re-01-q1`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:2,tags:[`real-estate`,`cap-rate`,`valuation`],concept:`what-it-is`,stem:`A building generates $6m of net operating income. The market cap rate for this asset type is 5.5%. What is its approximate value, in millions?`,answer:109.1,tolerance:1,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`$m`,inputHint:`Answer in millions of dollars, to 1 decimal place.`,explanation:`Value = NOI / cap rate = $6m / 0.055 = $109.1m. This is the perpetuity formula: an income stream divided by a required yield.`},{id:`alts-re-01-q2`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:3,tags:[`real-estate`,`cap-rate`,`sensitivity`],concept:`cap-rate-is-the-risk`,stem:`The same building, same $6m NOI, but the cap rate moves from 5.5% to 6.5%. By what percentage does the value fall?`,answer:15.4,tolerance:.5,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,inputHint:`Answer in percent, to 1 decimal place, as a positive number.`,explanation:`$6m/0.065 = $92.3m against $109.1m, a fall of 15.4%. The rent did not change and the tenants did not change. One point of cap rate did all of it, which is why real estate is far more rate-sensitive than its physical nature suggests.`},{id:`alts-re-01-q3`,type:`numeric`,difficulty:4,tags:[`real-estate`,`leverage`,`how-it-blows-up`],concept:`how-it-blows-up`,stem:`A property was bought for $100m with 60% loan-to-value debt. Its value falls 25%. By what percentage has the equity fallen?`,answer:62.5,tolerance:1,toleranceType:`abs`,unit:`%`,inputHint:`Answer in percent, as a positive number.`,explanation:`Equity was $40m against $60m of debt. Value falls to $75m, so equity is $15m — a fall of 62.5%. The building may be fully let and performing exactly as underwritten; leverage converted a 25% valuation move into a loss of nearly two thirds of the equity.`},{id:`alts-re-01-q4`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:3,tags:[`real-estate`,`styles`,`attribution`],concept:`the-styles`,stem:`A core real estate fund reports that 70% of its five-year return came from capital appreciation rather than income. How should you read that?`,choices:[`Strong asset management, since they added value to stabilised buildings`,`The return came largely from cap rate compression — a market outcome rather than what a core strategy is meant to deliver`,`The fund has been misclassified and is actually opportunistic`,`Nothing — the source of return is irrelevant if the total is good`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Core buildings are stabilised and well let by definition, which leaves limited room for asset management to create 70% of the return.`,`Correct. A core strategy is supposed to deliver mostly income. Capital appreciation on stabilised assets over five years is overwhelmingly cap rate compression, which is the market repricing rather than the manager performing.`,`The fund may well be a genuine core fund. The issue is that the market delivered the return, not that the label is wrong.`,`The source matters enormously for whether it repeats. Cap rate compression cannot continue indefinitely and reverses.`],explanation:`Attribution matters here for the same reason it matters in buyout: it distinguishes what the manager did from what the market did. A core fund carried by cap rate compression will look very different when cap rates expand.`},{id:`alts-re-01-q5`,type:`tfj`,difficulty:4,tags:[`real-estate`,`noi`,`cap-rate`],concept:`noi-quality`,stem:`"This building trades at a 7% cap rate against a 5% market average, so it is cheap." True or false — and why?`,isTrue:!1,justifications:[`False — a high cap rate can mean the market doubts the income will persist, through short leases, weak tenants or above-market rents`,`False — cap rates cannot be compared between buildings`,`True — a higher cap rate means more income per dollar of price, which is definitionally cheaper`,`False — because cap rates are set by appraisers rather than by the market`],justificationIndex:0,rationales:[`Correct. The cap rate is a required yield, and a high one usually reflects risk the buyer is being paid to take: leases expiring soon, a weak tenant, rents above market that will fall at renewal, or capital expenditure needed.`,`Cap rates are routinely and usefully compared. The point is to understand what a difference reflects.`,`This treats current NOI as durable. If that income is about to fall, more income per dollar today is not cheap.`,`Cap rates are derived from transaction evidence. Appraisers observe them rather than setting them.`],explanation:`The cap rate is the market's verdict on the income's durability as much as its size. Which is why the useful questions are behind the number — lease length, tenant credit, rents versus market, capex — rather than about the number itself.`},{id:`alts-re-01-q6`,type:`mcq`,difficulty:4,tags:[`real-estate`,`refinancing`,`on-the-job`],concept:`how-it-blows-up`,stem:`Why is the debt maturity profile of a real estate portfolio a distinct risk from the quality of its buildings?`,choices:[`Because lenders can demand repayment at any time`,`Because property debt is typically shorter than the hold period, so loans must be refinanced into whatever conditions happen to prevail`,`Because interest rates are fixed for the life of the loan`,`Because maturity determines the cap rate applied`],answerIndex:1,rationales:[`Term loans cannot generally be called at will. The risk is at maturity, not on demand.`,`Correct. A five-year loan on a ten-year hold has to be replaced. If it matures into higher rates and lower valuations, the required equity may not be available and a forced sale can follow — with nothing wrong with the building.`,`Some property debt is fixed and some floating, and either way the loan still matures and needs replacing.`,`Cap rates reflect required yields on the asset, not the owner's financing schedule.`],explanation:`This is a scheduling risk you can see years ahead, which makes it unusual among the risks in this domain. Mapping debt maturities against expected hold periods is straightforward diligence, and it is where portfolios of perfectly good buildings get into difficulty.`}],d=!1,f=null,p={schemaVersion:1,id:t,domain:n,title:r,summary:i,level:a,prereqs:o,estMinutes:10,examRelevance:c,lesson:l,questions:u,needsReview:!1,reviewNote:null};export{p as default,n as domain,s as estMinutes,c as examRelevance,t as id,l as lesson,a as level,d as needsReview,o as prereqs,u as questions,f as reviewNote,e as schemaVersion,i as summary,r as title};
